        "proof_strategy": "direct",
        "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $M=\\Gamma\\backslash\\mathbb H^3$ be a connected oriented complete finite-volume hyperbolic three-manifold with a compact smooth core and $k\\ge1$ disjoint standard torus cusp ends. Choose positively oriented peripheral bases $(\\mu_i,\\lambda_i)$. Let $\\rho_u$, for $u$ near $0$ in $\\mathbb C^k$, be the augmented holonomy family supplied by the augmented holonomy-coordinate theorem, based at the complete holonomy $\\rho_0$. Thus the logarithms satisfy $v_i(u)=\\tau_i(u)u_i$ with $\\operatorname{Im}\\tau_i(u)>0$.\n\nAfter shrinking this neighborhood, there are smooth hyperbolic metrics $g_u$ on $M$, possibly incomplete, with holonomy $\\rho_u$. They depend smoothly on the real and imaginary parts of $u$ on every compact subset, and $g_0$ is the given complete metric. Each end with $u_i=0$ is a complete finite-volume translation cusp. Each end with $u_i\\ne0$ has an explicit developing map onto the punctured tube about its peripheral axis, with the angular and axial periods specified by $u_i,v_i$.\n\nSuppose $J\\subseteq\\{1,\\ldots,k\\}$ and, for every $i\\in J$, a primitive integral pair $(p_i,q_i)$ satisfies\n$$p_i u_i+q_i v_i(u)=2\\pi i,$$\nwhile $u_i=0$ for $i\\notin J$. Then $g_u$ extends across all ends in $J$ to a smooth complete finite-volume hyperbolic metric on the ordinary Dehn filling whose meridians are $p_i\\mu_i+q_i\\lambda_i$. For arbitrary supplied orientation-reversing boundary homeomorphisms realizing these same slopes, the resulting topological filling is orientation-preservingly and core-preservingly homeomorphic to this realization, by a homeomorphism equal to the identity on the truncated original manifold. Transporting the realization's smooth structure and metric through this homeomorphism gives the asserted smooth hyperbolic metric on that supplied filling. No assertion is made about a separately prescribed smooth structure for an arbitrary topological gluing. The added cores are ordinary smooth geodesics, with no cone or orbifold singularity. Their lengths are\n$$\\ell_i=\\frac{|u_i|^2\\operatorname{Im}\\tau_i(u)}{2\\pi}>0.$$\nAlong such filling parameters tending to zero, the metrics converge smoothly to $g_0$ on every compact subset of $M$, the core lengths tend to zero, and the filled volumes tend to $\\operatorname{Vol}(M,g_0)$. No ideal triangulation is assumed. This theorem realizes given small holonomy solutions; it does not assert their existence for specified slopes.",
